welcome back so now we're ready to start  talking about some of the types of  machine learning and kind of categorize  the different methods of different  architectures we have to build models  from data and there are a lot of ways of  slicing this there's so many different  algorithms I mean there's so many just  you know so many flavors of neural  networks alone let alone all of the  other machine learning models but what  I'm gonna do is give you one of the most  kind of common breakdowns of types of  amount so we're talking about types of  nl and the two big distinctions in in  machine learning at least two of them  are whether or not your data or your  algorithm is supervised or unsupervised  so supervised learning means that you  have an expert who has labeled your data  with outcomes so you have labeled  training data and so so in some sense  this supervision comes in the form of  labels that tell you for this set of  inputs this is what the outcome or the  prediction is so I'll give you a very  concrete example let's say I have a  bunch of images of cats and dogs that's  my old dog so maybe I've got a bunch of  cats and a bunch of dogs and I have an  expert human go through and label all of  those you know cats dog cat dot so on  and so forth then a supervised learning  algorithm there is a whole family of  machine learning algorithms supervised  algorithms that will allow me to build a  classifier in so that in the future if I  have a picture of let's say I have a cat  in this case so if I have a picture of a  cat in the future the algorithm can  learn how to label that cat as the  supervisor would have the same way the  supervisor would have okay so that's  what we mean by supervised supervised  learning if your data comes in  categories like cats and dogs and birds  and people then this is called  classification so it's classification  if the data is kind of discrete data  categorical data or it's regression if  if the data is continuous so I give you  an example of classification an example  of regression would be let's say I give  you a bunch of airfoil designs okay so  I've got a bunch of airfoil designs  maybe you know my knack at double-o 12  and an epaulette and so on and so forth  and I measure what the drag is and let's  say the drag is a number it's a  continuous number like 0.3 or 0.4 and so  on and so forth what you would want to  do with a regression model is in the  future if I have a new airfoil I'd want  to be able to tell you oh that's going  to be a point three two drag coefficient  on that airfoil okay so this is  classification for discrete data like  categories dogs and cats or discrete  labels regression for continuously  varying labels like the drag coefficient  on an airfoil okay so that's one big  category of machine learning are  supervised algorithms and there are you  know classification and regression  techniques and another large category is  what we call unsupervised and as you  might have guessed there are no labels  so there's no expert telling you what's  what's different what's a cat what's a  dog what the drag coefficient is you  just get a bunch of data and you have to  make heads or tails of it or cats and  dogs of it okay  and so unsupervised machine learning is  is very interesting this is kind of what  we mean when we say data mining so  you've heard you know data mining we're  gonna take all of our mountains of data  and we're gonna mine it for patterns and  pull out interesting rules that we might  be beyond a human to write down so  that's what we mean here this is what  people talk about with data mining in  some sense if I gave it all of these  pictures of dogs and cats if I gave an  unsupervised Algar than all of these  pictures of dogs and cats but I  hold the algorithm there's two kind of  things you might want to be  distinguishing it might learn clusters  in the data it might learn  distinguishing features that tell dogs  and cats apart and it might learn that  there are these two categories that are  kind of different okay  and so that is is called clustering  again if you believe that your data is  discrete in some sense you will cluster  that data and then you could use those  clusters for a future classification and  then if it's continuous data like like  this case you might use something called  dimensionality reduction that's what I  call it dimension reduction pattern  extraction is another one pattern or  feature extraction pattern extraction  and I should say that each of these kind  of four groups here so classification  regression clustering and pattern  extraction or feature extraction each of  these four areas is a huge huge area of  machine learning there are text books on  classification and regression and  clustering and so on and so forth  thousands and thousands of papers and  researchers in each of these areas and  and that's something you have to think  about based on your data is do you have  labels does an expert has an expert  actually told you this is the thing I  want as an outcome and here's the number  for all of my input data okay a last  area and I'll talk about this in a  little bit I have kind of a slideshow  for for the next segment there's also a  group called semi-supervised and these  are algorithms where maybe there's some  expert feedback maybe you get in  reinforcement learning maybe my my robot  my agent gets a reward but it doesn't  get a reward after every action it takes  maybe after ten actions it gets one  reward so there's partial information it  gets from the environment or from a  supervisor but it has a lot of unlabeled  data as well and it has to kind of bring  these together so reinforcement learning  generative models really fit into the  semi supervised and this is actually  where a lot of interesting work is is  happening because you get some really  very useful models in the  semi-supervised world okay so more on  this soon with a flowchart Thanks
 

